WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:10.536
This is Chapter Two, Episode Six: the players in a democracy, and democracy in everyday life.

3
00:00:11.086 --> 00:00:13.686
There are many participants in a democracy.

4
00:00:13.636 --> 00:00:16.411
The voters are the foundation of the system.

5
00:00:16.411 --> 00:00:19.498
They are the ones who supply the politicians with power.

6
00:00:19.498 --> 00:00:26.736
In daily political life, the politicians and the parties play a central role when new legislation is to be passed.

7
00:00:26.736 --> 00:00:30.948
But there are many others who try to get influence on democratic decisions.

8
00:00:30.948 --> 00:00:35.411
Interest organisations and associations try to influence political life.

9
00:00:35.411 --> 00:00:39.849
The media also mean a lot for which questions get onto the political agenda.

10
00:00:39.849 --> 00:00:44.411
In addition, private business and the public sector also play a role.

11
00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:46.123
Let's meet them one by one.

12
00:00:46.123 --> 00:00:48.311
And here are the Danish names again.

13
00:00:48.311 --> 00:01:05.394
Socialdemokratiet, Venstre, Radikale Venstre, Det Konservative Folkeparti, SF, Moderaterne, Liberal Alliance, Dansk Folkeparti, Enhedslisten, Alternativet, Danmarksdemokraterne and Borgernes Parti.

14
00:01:05.944 --> 00:01:07.707
First, the voters.

15
00:01:07.657 --> 00:01:13.869
It is the basic principle of democracy that all power comes from the people, that is, the voters.

16
00:01:13.869 --> 00:01:16.719
And there is a hard fight for the voters' favour.

17
00:01:16.719 --> 00:01:20.748
Fewer and fewer voters vote for the same party all their lives.

18
00:01:21.298 --> 00:01:30.661
Sixty years ago, most voters voted for a party that matched their work and their class in society, and that looked after their economic interests in particular.

19
00:01:30.611 --> 00:01:40.923
Workers typically voted for the Social Democrats, farmers voted for Venstre, and the self-employed and the middle class typically voted for the Conservative People's Party.

20
00:01:40.923 --> 00:01:45.011
Voters were loyal to their party and to the party's ideology.

21
00:01:45.011 --> 00:01:52.386
They often voted for the same party every single time, almost regardless of how the party acted in the political system.

22
00:01:52.386 --> 00:01:54.473
That is no longer the case.

23
00:01:54.473 --> 00:01:57.386
Voters change party far more often today.

24
00:01:57.386 --> 00:02:04.206
Voters also vote more on the basis of their views on questions that do not necessarily have to do with their own economic interests.

25
00:02:04.756 --> 00:02:09.119
That can be views on individual issues that are politically topical right now.

26
00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:16.494
These can concern health, immigration, climate, how hard crime should be punished, or something completely different.

27
00:02:16.494 --> 00:02:19.744
On the other hand, there are still patterns in voting.

28
00:02:19.744 --> 00:02:23.181
Privately employed people vote more for centre-right parties.

29
00:02:23.181 --> 00:02:28.819
Publicly employed people vote more for the Social Democrats and other parties on the left wing.

30
00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:34.044
Men vote more for centre-right parties, and women more often for parties on the left wing.

31
00:02:34.044 --> 00:02:48.072
Venstre and the Denmark Democrats are often strong in the rural areas, while SF, the Red-Green Alliance, the Social Liberal Party and the Alternative have most of their voters in Copenhagen and other of the biggest cities.

32
00:02:48.622 --> 00:02:51.097
Second, the political parties.

33
00:02:51.047 --> 00:02:56.234
The political parties are probably the most important participants in Danish democracy.

34
00:02:56.234 --> 00:03:00.597
At elections, the political fight takes place between the parties.

35
00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:06.334
In the choice between different political parties, voters can express their political views.

36
00:03:06.334 --> 00:03:14.997
The parties act in that way as mouthpieces for the population's different political opinions, but they also have great influence on voters' opinions.

37
00:03:14.997 --> 00:03:19.484
When parties announce certain political views, it influences the voters.

38
00:03:19.484 --> 00:03:25.578
In the daily political work too, the parties play a crucial role in the ongoing political debate.

39
00:03:26.128 --> 00:03:29.590
The political parties are not mentioned in the constitution.

40
00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:36.640
When Denmark got its first constitution in 1849, there were no political parties as we know them today.

41
00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.853
A member of the Rigsdag was to serve the whole people and not be bound by special interests.

42
00:03:41.853 --> 00:03:52.068
But gradually, as democracy developed in the second half of the 1800s and the beginning of the 1900s, political interest groups and parties arose.

43
00:03:52.618 --> 00:03:56.668
As a citizen, you can be a member of one of the political parties.

44
00:03:56.618 --> 00:04:03.068
Around 1950, about 650,000 people were members of a political party.

45
00:04:03.068 --> 00:04:08.405
That corresponded to almost one in four of those with the right to vote in general elections.

46
00:04:08.405 --> 00:04:11.705
Since then, the number has fallen considerably.

47
00:04:11.705 --> 00:04:16.393
In 2024, the number was about 106,000.

48
00:04:16.393 --> 00:04:21.680
That corresponds to about one in forty of those with the right to vote in general elections.

49
00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:30.930
The biggest party, the Social Democrats, has about 27,000 members, and the second biggest, Venstre, about 20,000.

50
00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:39.830
It is the parties' members who decide whom a party should nominate as candidates, and thereby which candidates the rest of the population can vote for at an election.

51
00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:49.422
Even though the political parties have fewer members, they are still very important participants in democratic life, and they also have great influence on voters' opinions.

52
00:04:49.972 --> 00:04:52.497
Third, the members of the Folketing.

53
00:04:52.447 --> 00:05:00.447
A member of the Folketing is bound only by their personal conviction, and not by any instruction from the voters or from their parties.

54
00:05:00.447 --> 00:05:03.109
This is what the Danish constitution says.

55
00:05:03.109 --> 00:05:07.022
It means that the member in principle only represents themself.

56
00:05:07.022 --> 00:05:13.392
They can therefore vote against their party's official policy, for example if it conflicts with their personal views.

57
00:05:13.942 --> 00:05:19.417
As a main rule, however, individual politicians follow their party's political line.

58
00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:24.554
In some cases, a party in the Folketing decides that its members are set free.

59
00:05:24.554 --> 00:05:28.429
In those cases, the party does not set a common position.

60
00:05:28.429 --> 00:05:32.329
Most often this happens in cases about morals and ethics.

61
00:05:32.329 --> 00:05:38.970
That could for example be in discussions about abortion, artificial insemination or the rights of homosexuals.

62
00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:46.007
A member of the Folketing can also choose to leave their party if they no longer feel in agreement with the party's views.

63
00:05:45.957 --> 00:05:52.082
In that case, the person can become either a loner, a løsgænger, or a member of another party.

64
00:05:52.082 --> 00:05:58.532
But they keep their membership of the Folketing until the next general election and can continue to take part in votes.

65
00:05:58.532 --> 00:06:03.972
The Speaker of the Folketing, Folketingets formand, is Søren Gade from Venstre.

66
00:06:04.522 --> 00:06:07.122
Now it is time for your exam practice.

67
00:06:07.072 --> 00:06:11.984
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

68
00:06:11.984 --> 00:06:19.470
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

69
00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:21.270
Question 1.

70
00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:25.620
Which party did farmers typically vote for in the middle of the 1900s?

71
00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.645
Option A: The Danish People's Party.

72
00:06:28.645 --> 00:06:30.857
Option B: The Greens.

73
00:06:30.857 --> 00:06:32.952
Option C: Venstre.
[DA] Hvilket parti stemte landmænd typisk på i midten af 1900-tallet?  A: Dansk Folkeparti  |  B: De grønne  |  C: Venstre

74
00:06:39.502 --> 00:06:41.702
The answer is C: Venstre.

75
00:06:41.652 --> 00:06:45.690
It appeared in the June 2020 and May 2024 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Venstre

76
00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.490
Question 2.

77
00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.840
Which party did workers typically vote for in the middle of the 1900s?

78
00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.877
Option A: Venstre.

79
00:06:53.877 --> 00:06:58.115
Option B: The Social Democrats, Socialdemokratiet.

80
00:06:58.115 --> 00:07:01.092
Option C: The Social Liberal Party.
[DA] Hvilket parti stemte arbejdere typisk på i midten af 1900-tallet?  A: Venstre  |  B: Socialdemokratiet  |  C: Radikale Venstre

81
00:07:07.642 --> 00:07:12.029
The answer is B: The Social Democrats, Socialdemokratiet.

82
00:07:11.979 --> 00:07:16.254
It appeared in the November 2020 and June 2022 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Socialdemokratiet

83
00:07:16.804 --> 00:07:18.141
Question 3.

84
00:07:18.091 --> 00:07:22.741
How have the political parties' membership numbers developed overall since 1950?

85
00:07:22.741 --> 00:07:25.029
Option A: They have fallen.

86
00:07:25.029 --> 00:07:27.096
Option B: They have risen.
[DA] Hvordan har de politiske partiers medlemstal overordnet udviklet sig siden 1950?  A: De er faldet  |  B: De er steget

87
00:07:33.646 --> 00:07:36.083
The answer is A: They have fallen.

88
00:07:36.033 --> 00:07:41.058
It appeared in 4 different exams, most recently in June 2026.
[DA] ✔ A: De er faldet

89
00:07:41.608 --> 00:07:44.058
That's the end of Part One of Episode 6.

90
00:07:44.008 --> 00:07:51.133
In Part Two, we continue with interest organisations, the media, and democracy in everyday life.

91
00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:52.188
See you there.

